ust be closing  stores as leases came up because retail  is you know the leverage is so strong  that relatively small decreases in sales  wipe out all your profitability very  quickly so it's a it's a very thin  business so it but I think it is you  know much easy to look back now with all  the data then then when you're looking  forward but best buy I was on a roll and  they had the financial strength to  underwrite the sort of revamping of the  stores that we didn't have the capital  to do so it was struck us as at the time  as sort of the best option for everybody  though it's you know everybody should go  through a merger being acquired once you  learn it's an incredible education of I  think the danger of almost every  acquisition is even if they're done for  strategic purposes and you acquire  because you want the skills and gifts of  the group you're acquiring there's an ax  jurors bias that I've seen whether I was  in the investment banking side or or in  the operating side where the acquire  tends to think they have all the answers  and so they think best buy made some  major strategic mistakes by not fully  understanding what the assets they were  buying before they put all the changes  in place but it's I think it's a it's a  constant juggling act of all those  stakeholders  well thanks also for coming I think a  lot of times ministry organizations have  a lot of difficulty in the management of  people how they hire how they fire how  they grow many times they can't fire  just out of obligation or out of you  know some can you just kind of talk  about how you've managed habitat if you  see some of those issues when you came  in and how are you gonna grow and build  a business going forward  thank you it's it's a really interesting  challenge I think and actually it was so  that's part of why I'm so valuable and  to work in a church environment before  coming to Habitat which and if you ever  if you want the most complex management  town in some ways try t